1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,388
August 1951. A nameless ridge north of Yanggu,

2
00:00:04,408 --> 00:00:07,176
deep in the mountains of eastern Korea.

3
00:00:07,264 --> 00:00:09,350
After days and nights of shelling,

4
00:00:09,370 --> 00:00:12,873
not a single tree was left standing on its crest.

5
00:00:13,260 --> 00:00:15,346
Yet the moment the guns fell silent,

6
00:00:15,366 --> 00:00:17,019
enemy machine guns opened

7
00:00:17,039 --> 00:00:18,851
up again from the bare earth.

8
00:00:18,974 --> 00:00:20,867
Bunkers buried deep in the ridge had

9
00:00:20,887 --> 00:00:23,395
ridden out the entire bombardment.

10
00:00:23,781 --> 00:00:25,595
In the end, men had to crawl up the

11
00:00:25,615 --> 00:00:27,757
slopes with rifles and grenades.

12
00:00:27,897 --> 00:00:29,583
For every bunker they destroyed,

13
00:00:29,603 --> 00:00:31,471
someone fell on that slope.

14
00:00:32,020 --> 00:00:33,610
For this one ridge alone,

15
00:00:33,630 --> 00:00:36,603
South Korean and UN casualties would climb

16
00:00:36,623 --> 00:00:39,004
past two thousand eight hundred.

17
00:00:39,102 --> 00:00:40,870
An American military newspaper

18
00:00:40,890 --> 00:00:42,803
gave it a chilling name.

19
00:00:43,187 --> 00:00:44,498
Bloody Ridge.

20
00:00:44,614 --> 00:00:46,882
This is the story of a three-week battle

21
00:00:46,902 --> 00:00:49,428
fought in the shadow of the truce talks.

22
00:00:49,821 --> 00:00:52,261
By the summer of 1951, the war had

23
00:00:52,281 --> 00:00:55,596
ground to a halt near the 38th Parallel.

24
00:00:55,664 --> 00:00:59,093
On July 10th, truce talks finally opened at

25
00:00:59,113 --> 00:01:02,433
Kaesong, an old city near the front line.

26
00:01:02,826 --> 00:01:04,688
But while the negotiators haggled,

27
00:01:04,708 --> 00:01:07,045
the guns at the front never stopped.

28
00:01:07,201 --> 00:01:08,764
Every yard of ground held

29
00:01:08,784 --> 00:01:10,483
was leverage at the table.

30
00:01:10,879 --> 00:01:12,867
The UN's main line of resistance

31
00:01:12,887 --> 00:01:14,745
was called the Kansas Line.

32
00:01:14,804 --> 00:01:17,242
In the rugged mountains north of Yanggu,

33
00:01:17,262 --> 00:01:20,516
the US 2nd Infantry Division held the line.

34
00:01:20,906 --> 00:01:23,557
The problem was a ridge just to the north.

35
00:01:23,696 --> 00:01:25,634
North Korean observers hidden on

36
00:01:25,654 --> 00:01:27,079
it were calling down accurate

37
00:01:27,099 --> 00:01:29,745
artillery fire on UN positions.

38
00:01:30,141 --> 00:01:31,976
Eighth Army commander General James

39
00:01:31,996 --> 00:01:34,343
Van Fleet ordered the ridge taken.

40
00:01:34,512 --> 00:01:36,449
He wanted the enemy's eyes put out,

41
00:01:36,469 --> 00:01:39,294
and South Korean units seasoned in battle.

42
00:01:39,705 --> 00:01:42,454
In late July, the US 38th Infantry

43
00:01:42,474 --> 00:01:44,184
had set up a patrol base on

44
00:01:44,204 --> 00:01:48,406
Hill 1179, Taeu-san, to the east.

45
00:01:48,473 --> 00:01:50,377
But heavy monsoon rains washed

46
00:01:50,397 --> 00:01:52,392
out the roads, and the main attack

47
00:01:52,412 --> 00:01:54,626
was put off until mid-August.

48
00:01:55,018 --> 00:01:57,035
The objective: three peaks on

49
00:01:57,055 --> 00:01:59,118
a ridge running east to west.

50
00:01:59,173 --> 00:02:04,684
From west to east, Hills 983, 940, and 773.

51
00:02:05,086 --> 00:02:08,372
Hill 983, the highest of the three, looked

52
00:02:08,392 --> 00:02:11,581
straight down on the UN lines to the south.

53
00:02:11,701 --> 00:02:13,388
The ridge was knife-edge narrow,

54
00:02:13,408 --> 00:02:16,573
with sheer slopes falling away on both sides.

55
00:02:16,968 --> 00:02:19,002
The North Koreans had studded the ridge with

56
00:02:19,022 --> 00:02:21,960
log bunkers buried under earth and rock.

57
00:02:22,115 --> 00:02:24,121
US records say the largest could

58
00:02:24,141 --> 00:02:26,142
hold as many as sixty men.

59
00:02:26,546 --> 00:02:28,958
Trenches linked bunker to bunker,

60
00:02:28,978 --> 00:02:31,837
and minefields covered the approaches.

61
00:02:31,896 --> 00:02:34,721
When the shells came, they took cover; when

62
00:02:34,741 --> 00:02:38,036
the shelling lifted, the guns came back out.

63
00:02:38,411 --> 00:02:43,055
On August 18th, 1951, the ROK 36th Regiment

64
00:02:43,075 --> 00:02:45,899
of the 5th Division led the first assault.

65
00:02:46,009 --> 00:02:47,571
It was a South Korean regiment

66
00:02:47,591 --> 00:02:49,918
attached to the US 2nd Division.

67
00:02:50,315 --> 00:02:51,505
Before the assault,

68
00:02:51,525 --> 00:02:53,962
UN artillery pounded the ridge.

69
00:02:54,086 --> 00:02:55,784
Several artillery battalions,

70
00:02:55,804 --> 00:02:58,448
heavy mortars, and tanks: every gun

71
00:02:58,468 --> 00:03:00,781
in range was brought to bear.

72
00:03:01,168 --> 00:03:03,083
Days of shelling turned the green

73
00:03:03,103 --> 00:03:05,564
ridge into bare, pale earth.

74
00:03:05,660 --> 00:03:07,952
But when the barrage lifted, the enemy

75
00:03:07,972 --> 00:03:10,489
guns came back to life underground.

76
00:03:10,875 --> 00:03:13,746
As the attackers climbed, the defenders threw

77
00:03:13,766 --> 00:03:16,568
and rolled grenades down on their heads.

78
00:03:16,692 --> 00:03:17,889
With every step upward,

79
00:03:17,909 --> 00:03:20,667
more grenades came tumbling down.

80
00:03:21,065 --> 00:03:22,775
After five days of repeated

81
00:03:22,795 --> 00:03:25,268
frontal assaults, the 36th Regiment

82
00:03:25,288 --> 00:03:27,475
finally stood on the ridge.

83
00:03:27,593 --> 00:03:28,893
But a fierce counterattack

84
00:03:28,913 --> 00:03:30,357
threw them back off it.

85
00:03:30,746 --> 00:03:33,158
As casualties mounted, the exhausted

86
00:03:33,178 --> 00:03:35,406
men's morale began to crack.

87
00:03:35,433 --> 00:03:37,803
On August 27th, some units of the

88
00:03:37,823 --> 00:03:40,548
36th Regiment broke and fell back.

89
00:03:40,939 --> 00:03:42,463
The panic spread to elements of the

90
00:03:42,483 --> 00:03:45,457
US 9th Infantry, sent up in support.

91
00:03:45,562 --> 00:03:48,775
That same day, the 9th's 2nd Battalion struck

92
00:03:48,795 --> 00:03:52,456
at Hill 983, and it too was forced back.

93
00:03:52,845 --> 00:03:56,608
The next day, the 3rd Battalion went for Hill 773,

94
00:03:56,628 --> 00:03:59,192
and was driven off by a night attack.

95
00:03:59,290 --> 00:04:00,715
It was the attackers who were being

96
00:04:00,735 --> 00:04:03,744
worn down, one battalion at a time.

97
00:04:04,143 --> 00:04:06,697
On August 30th, the 9th Infantry hit

98
00:04:06,717 --> 00:04:10,281
Hill 940, in the center, head-on.

99
00:04:10,341 --> 00:04:11,539
They got within a few hundred

100
00:04:11,559 --> 00:04:13,576
yards of the crest before bunker

101
00:04:13,596 --> 00:04:15,655
machine guns stopped them again.

102
00:04:16,036 --> 00:04:18,522
Company A was cut to half strength,

103
00:04:18,542 --> 00:04:21,969
and its commander, Lieutenant Dunn, was killed.

104
00:04:22,096 --> 00:04:24,523
Lieutenant Morrow, the artillery observer

105
00:04:24,543 --> 00:04:27,376
beside him, took command on the spot.

106
00:04:27,756 --> 00:04:29,272
And so the ridge was soaked

107
00:04:29,292 --> 00:04:31,366
in the blood of both sides.

108
00:04:31,427 --> 00:04:33,547
The military newspaper Stars and

109
00:04:33,567 --> 00:04:36,129
Stripes called it Bloody Ridge.

110
00:04:36,177 --> 00:04:37,716
For security it never named the

111
00:04:37,736 --> 00:04:39,275
place, so the men fighting

112
00:04:39,295 --> 00:04:41,010
there didn't know it was them.

113
00:04:41,397 --> 00:04:44,258
On August 28th, the new X Corps commander,

114
00:04:44,278 --> 00:04:47,535
General Clovis Byers, changed the plan.

115
00:04:47,590 --> 00:04:49,323
Instead of hammering one ridge,

116
00:04:49,343 --> 00:04:51,293
the whole corps front would push forward

117
00:04:51,313 --> 00:04:53,959
at once, starting August 31st.

118
00:04:54,352 --> 00:04:56,535
The ROK 5th and 1st Marine

119
00:04:56,555 --> 00:04:58,846
Divisions drove on the Punchbowl;

120
00:04:58,866 --> 00:05:01,793
the ROK 7th, west of the ridge.

121
00:05:01,913 --> 00:05:03,611
The enemy could no longer mass his

122
00:05:03,631 --> 00:05:05,862
guns and reserves in one place.

123
00:05:06,256 --> 00:05:08,641
On August 31st, the Marines met

124
00:05:08,661 --> 00:05:10,098
little resistance and reached

125
00:05:10,118 --> 00:05:12,237
the Punchbowl's northern rim.

126
00:05:12,333 --> 00:05:14,111
The flank of the North Koreans on the

127
00:05:14,131 --> 00:05:16,646
ridge was slowly being laid bare.

128
00:05:17,043 --> 00:05:20,131
Meanwhile, the 1st Battalion, 9th Infantry,

129
00:05:20,151 --> 00:05:23,886
broke the bunkers on Hill 773 one by one.

130
00:05:24,012 --> 00:05:26,279
One platoon leader, his radio dead,

131
00:05:26,299 --> 00:05:28,731
took a bunker with nothing but grenades.

132
00:05:29,128 --> 00:05:32,138
On September 3rd, after napalm and bombs

133
00:05:32,158 --> 00:05:35,451
swept the ridge, the attack went in again.

134
00:05:35,568 --> 00:05:38,417
Company C, about eighty-five men strong,

135
00:05:38,437 --> 00:05:40,573
came out of it with about thirty.

136
00:05:40,983 --> 00:05:44,270
Then the US 23rd and 38th Infantry swung

137
00:05:44,290 --> 00:05:46,625
wide around both ends of the ridge.

138
00:05:46,724 --> 00:05:48,389
With the 9th still pressing the

139
00:05:48,409 --> 00:05:50,637
front, it was a double envelopment

140
00:05:50,657 --> 00:05:52,619
closing from three sides.

141
00:05:53,009 --> 00:05:55,649
On September 4th and 5th, the 2nd Division

142
00:05:55,669 --> 00:05:58,085
finally reached the top of the ridge.

143
00:05:58,153 --> 00:06:00,428
But the crest was strangely quiet.

144
00:06:00,812 --> 00:06:02,304
The North Koreans had already

145
00:06:02,324 --> 00:06:03,953
slipped away to the north.

146
00:06:04,072 --> 00:06:06,191
Left behind on the ridge were abandoned

147
00:06:06,211 --> 00:06:08,605
supplies and more than five hundred

148
00:06:08,625 --> 00:06:11,168
enemy dead, lying where they fell.

149
00:06:11,560 --> 00:06:13,309
Per the US Army official history,

150
00:06:13,329 --> 00:06:17,417
South Korean and UN casualties topped 2,800.

151
00:06:17,508 --> 00:06:20,572
By Korean records, the ROK 36th Regiment

152
00:06:20,592 --> 00:06:23,167
alone lost more than a thousand men.

153
00:06:23,265 --> 00:06:25,259
The 2nd Division estimated enemy

154
00:06:25,279 --> 00:06:27,629
losses at over fifteen thousand.

155
00:06:27,994 --> 00:06:30,631
Why did one ridge cost so much blood?

156
00:06:30,723 --> 00:06:33,179
Let's break it down into four factors.

157
00:06:33,576 --> 00:06:36,109
First, the underground fortress.

158
00:06:36,510 --> 00:06:38,696
The bunkers were heavy timbers piled with

159
00:06:38,716 --> 00:06:41,860
layer upon layer of earth and stone.

160
00:06:41,988 --> 00:06:44,027
Unless a heavy shell landed squarely

161
00:06:44,047 --> 00:06:46,568
on top, they would not collapse.

162
00:06:46,951 --> 00:06:49,255
Second, the limits of firepower.

163
00:06:49,646 --> 00:06:52,308
The UN owned the sky and the artillery.

164
00:06:52,363 --> 00:06:55,700
The 155-millimeter Long Tom was the best

165
00:06:55,720 --> 00:06:58,364
bunker-buster, but there were few of them.

166
00:06:58,479 --> 00:07:00,543
Digging the enemy out of his bunkers was,

167
00:07:00,563 --> 00:07:02,990
in the end, a job for the infantry.

168
00:07:03,387 --> 00:07:06,257
Third, the price of the frontal assault.

169
00:07:06,648 --> 00:07:08,122
On a ridge that narrow,

170
00:07:08,142 --> 00:07:10,205
there was no room to spread out.

171
00:07:10,315 --> 00:07:12,457
Men climbing in single file walked

172
00:07:12,477 --> 00:07:14,596
straight into the bunkers' fire.

173
00:07:14,685 --> 00:07:16,623
Some platoons went into the attack

174
00:07:16,643 --> 00:07:18,399
made up mostly of replacements

175
00:07:18,419 --> 00:07:20,784
who had only just arrived.

176
00:07:21,181 --> 00:07:23,526
Fourth, the double envelopment.

177
00:07:23,912 --> 00:07:25,631
In the end, the ridge was opened not

178
00:07:25,651 --> 00:07:28,158
from the front, but from the flanks.

179
00:07:28,281 --> 00:07:30,719
Squeezed along the whole front and from

180
00:07:30,739 --> 00:07:33,792
both flanks, the enemy gave it up himself.

181
00:07:34,202 --> 00:07:35,921
But the North Koreans had fallen back

182
00:07:35,941 --> 00:07:38,187
only a few miles, to fortified

183
00:07:38,207 --> 00:07:40,804
positions on another ridge to the north.

184
00:07:40,864 --> 00:07:43,905
And on September 13th, the 2nd Division went

185
00:07:43,925 --> 00:07:46,864
on the attack again, toward that ridge.

186
00:07:47,259 --> 00:07:48,476
That place would come to be

187
00:07:48,496 --> 00:07:50,867
called Heartbreak Ridge.

188
00:07:50,916 --> 00:07:52,490
Bloody Ridge was only the beginning of

189
00:07:52,510 --> 00:07:55,008
a longer, harsher war for the hills.

190
00:07:55,402 --> 00:07:57,868
Today, a memorial to the Battle of Bloody

191
00:07:57,888 --> 00:08:01,737
Ridge stands in Worun-ri, Yanggu County.

192
00:08:01,833 --> 00:08:03,885
On those slopes, the search for those who

193
00:08:03,905 --> 00:08:06,882
never came home has gone on for years.

194
00:08:07,428 --> 00:08:09,938
A single line on the negotiating table

195
00:08:09,958 --> 00:08:12,908
cost thousands of lives on this ridge.

196
00:08:13,014 --> 00:08:15,726
The ground they held is today's Yanggu,

197
00:08:15,746 --> 00:08:17,320
just south of the armistice

198
00:08:17,340 --> 00:08:19,455
line that still divides Korea.

199
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:23,043
A nameless ridge north of Yanggu.

200
00:08:23,131 --> 00:08:26,199
To this day, its name remains Bloody Ridge.

201
00:08:26,572 --> 00:08:28,266
If this story meant something to you,

202
00:08:28,286 --> 00:08:30,644
please subscribe and like the video.

203
00:08:30,664 --> 00:08:31,772
We'll continue to bring the

204
00:08:31,792 --> 00:08:33,720
forgotten records of war to light.
